    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d362">

                <pb facs="0559" n="273" sameAs="frus:frus1916Supp/pg273"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 841.711/593</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Germany</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Gerard</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Berlin</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1916-06-16T14:00:00+02:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">June 16, 1916, 2 p. m.</hi>
                        </date>
                        <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received June 17, 4 p. m.</hi>]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>4007. Note to the Allies of May 24 relative to seizure and detention of the
                        mails<anchor n="2" xml:id="d362-f1"/> published in full in the German press.
                    It is very favorably received by the moderate papers although <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Frankfurter Zeitung</hi> cannot agree to admit
                    securities, checks, coupons, etc., are to be considered not as mail matter but
                    merchandise. Radical papers discount the note and claim that the time has come
                    for the threat which they consider is contained in the last paragraph of the
                    German note of May 4 to be carried into effect.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Gerard</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
